WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:01.130
Good morning, Hank, It's Tuesday.

00:00:01.130 --> 00:00:03.660
I woke up at 4:10 AM on Sunday and got into
a car with Sarah.

00:00:03.660 --> 00:00:04.750
How you feelin' Sarah?

00:00:04.750 --> 00:00:05.750
Sarah: Poorly.

00:00:05.750 --> 00:00:09.100
John: And drove through the dark to the Indianapolis
Airport, got through security and picked up

00:00:09.100 --> 00:00:12.660
The New York Times because my editor and publisher,
Julie Strauss Gabel, was profiled in it.

00:00:12.660 --> 00:00:16.030
Then I took a brief nap which Sarah was kind
enough to video, got on the plane, read a

00:00:16.030 --> 00:00:19.980
great profile of Toni Morrison, and enjoyed
the view, and five short hours later, we landed

00:00:19.980 --> 00:00:23.939
in Los Angeles, where, of course, sunshine
and blue skies, because: drought.

00:00:23.939 --> 00:00:27.689
Got a car to the hotel, took a nap which ended
when you called me, opened the window to briefly

00:00:27.689 --> 00:00:31.119
contemplate the role of the palm tree in the
California landscape, but then I got distracted

00:00:31.119 --> 00:00:32.680
by the huge pile of books in my room.

00:00:32.680 --> 00:00:35.150
I wasn't sure what they were for but I signed
them out of habit.

00:00:35.150 --> 00:00:37.930
Lunch came via room service, but we were tight
on time, so I had to get in the bath.

00:00:37.930 --> 00:00:42.010
I know I could have saved time by showering,
but showers are disgusting; having water droplets

00:00:42.010 --> 00:00:45.480
pelted at you like an attack from above is
just uncivilized.

00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:48.530
Plus there's the indignity of not being able
to eat while getting clean.

00:00:48.530 --> 00:00:52.520
I ironed my shirt, got my make-up done -- God,
I love make-up! -- and prepared for the MTV

00:00:52.520 --> 00:00:53.520
Movie Awards.

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:55.780
I'm sorry Hank, I was about to tell you that
I'm dressed, but, in fact I'm not because

00:00:55.780 --> 00:00:57.910
there's been a sock emergency: we're changing
socks.

00:00:57.910 --> 00:00:59.160
Now I am dressed.

00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:02.830
Also, I've switched to the iPhone camera for
subtlety, so sorry about the shaking.

00:01:02.830 --> 00:01:06.340
We met up with Elise and drove past yet more
palm trees to a different hotel where there

00:01:06.340 --> 00:01:07.900
was Justice Smith a.k.a.

00:01:07.900 --> 00:01:09.060
Radar,
How are you?

00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:10.719
Justice Smith: I'm so good!

00:01:10.719 --> 00:01:13.649
Also Alex Wolff and Nat Wolff, seen here getting
his hair done.

00:01:13.649 --> 00:01:15.399
And then we drove to the awards.

00:01:15.399 --> 00:01:16.399
Fans:
*Screaming*

00:01:16.399 --> 00:01:19.049
John: Maybe at some point that will stop being
weird, but not yet.

00:01:19.049 --> 00:01:23.249
Hank, it's difficult to express the intense
and overwhelming strangeness of this stuff.

00:01:23.249 --> 00:01:27.840
I mean I was happy to be there, and happier
and more grateful still to be there with friends

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:31.030
and family, but in this moment, one feels
that these photographers are imagining you

00:01:31.030 --> 00:01:35.979
as an object that they need to acquire rather
than, like, an actual human being.

00:01:35.979 --> 00:01:36.979
How does this feel, Alex?

00:01:36.979 --> 00:01:37.979
Alex Wolff: Terrifying.

00:01:37.979 --> 00:01:40.759
John: And, in turn, it's very difficult to
feel like they are actual people, like the

00:01:40.759 --> 00:01:43.770
kind who, as Whitman beautifully put it, "contain
multitudes".

00:01:43.770 --> 00:01:48.180
Instead, it feels like they are objects, clicking
cameras, and your overriding concern is that,

00:01:48.180 --> 00:01:52.219
in the brief photographic intersection of
light and time, the object version of you

00:01:52.219 --> 00:01:56.820
that they capture is not, like, picking its
nose or doing that weird thing with its smile

00:01:56.820 --> 00:01:57.990
it sometimes does.

00:01:57.990 --> 00:02:01.090
But then it was over, and Cara Delevingne
showed up and she always makes me feel better

00:02:01.090 --> 00:02:04.539
at this stuff because she knows just how to
handle it all, and she and Nat and I got to

00:02:04.539 --> 00:02:08.750
introduce a clip from Paper Towns of Q and
Margo Nair-ing off Chuck's eyebrow.

00:02:08.750 --> 00:02:12.110
And then we were rushed in to the show itself
which was really fun, except the whole time

00:02:12.110 --> 00:02:16.200
I was distracted by this giant, thin-legged,
popcorn bucket that seemed to be staring at

00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:17.970
me in a vaguely lascivious way.

00:02:17.970 --> 00:02:21.810
I'd be like, "Oh this is fun, I'm havin' fun,
oh yeah, I could dance a little bit," and

00:02:21.810 --> 00:02:23.970
then God that giant popcorn is still looking
at me!

00:02:23.970 --> 00:02:27.031
But anyway, Shailene won for best actress
for her role in The Fault in Our Stars, and

00:02:27.031 --> 00:02:30.030
she was so kind about me in her acceptance
speech that she made me cry.

00:02:30.030 --> 00:02:32.970
And then during the commercial break I was
still crying and Nat told me, "This would

00:02:32.970 --> 00:02:37.770
be really touching if you didn't cry over,
like, a good meal," which, you know, fair

00:02:37.770 --> 00:02:38.770
enough.

00:02:38.770 --> 00:02:41.250
And then, The Fault in Our Stars won for best
movie, and everyone had told me that, if we

00:02:41.250 --> 00:02:44.600
won, I should make the acceptance speech so
that I could thank Esther, and I got to, and

00:02:44.600 --> 00:02:49.010
it was amazing, and I know it's not the Oscars
or anything, but whatever; it was our Oscars,

00:02:49.010 --> 00:02:53.390
and it was so great to be there with Nat and
Justice and Cara and Shai, and to have this

00:02:53.390 --> 00:02:57.670
moment to feel grateful for The Fault in Our
Stars at the same time as I could feel excited

00:02:57.670 --> 00:02:58.670
about Paper Towns.

00:02:58.670 --> 00:03:02.460
Right, so go back to those famous L.A. palm
trees, for a second; they are not native to

00:03:02.460 --> 00:03:06.810
Los Angeles, and, in fact, can't survive there
without copious amounts of watering.

00:03:06.810 --> 00:03:12.540
They're cool and beautiful and totally unsustainable
and unnatural, much like the whole screaming

00:03:12.540 --> 00:03:14.180
machine of celebrity.

00:03:14.180 --> 00:03:18.280
But later at a restaurant with my friends,
like, the real complicated, multitudinous

00:03:18.280 --> 00:03:22.570
people who made The Fault in Our Stars and
Paper Towns, I kept thinking about how we

00:03:22.570 --> 00:03:25.790
actually care about each other and hold each
other up.

00:03:25.790 --> 00:03:29.830
Not just when the cameras are off, but especially
when the cameras are off.

00:03:29.830 --> 00:03:34.890
And then my friend and movie producer, Isaac,
made me turn my camera off which was the best

00:03:34.890 --> 00:03:38.520
part of a very good and very long day.

00:03:38.520 --> 00:03:40.260
Hank, I'll see you on Friday.

